    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, paragraph containing equation (1.1)",
      "anchor": "q : R × R → R^m; subscripts are used to denote partial derivatives",
      "observation": "The ambient variables are real spatial position x and time t. The scalar case has m = 1. Subscripts t and x denote the corresponding partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, paragraph immediately before equation (1.2)",
      "anchor": "one-dimensional pipe at constant velocity ū",
      "observation": "The example concerns a contaminant transported by fluid with constant velocity ū; q(x,t) represents its concentration or density."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, equation (1.2)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + ū q_x(x,t) = 0",
      "observation": "The stated scalar advection equation has constant transport coefficient ū and zero right-hand side."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, equation (1.3) and adjoining sentences",
      "anchor": "admits solutions of the form q(x,t) = q̃(x − ūt), for any function q̃(ξ)",
      "observation": "The source asserts that translating a profile by ūt produces a solution of equation (1.2), and describes the profile as arbitrary without an explicit regularity qualification."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, sentence following equation (1.3)",
      "anchor": "propagates with constant speed ū and unchanged shape",
      "observation": "The source interprets the formula as propagation of the concentration profile or waveform without a change of shape."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "Constant real scalar transport velocity ū, inherited from the real scalar case and the constant-velocity pipe example (printed page 1, scalar-case paragraph and paragraph preceding (1.2)).",
      "Profile q̃ of one real variable ξ, universally described as 'any function'; its real scalar values are inherited from the scalar q context (printed page 1, (1.1), scalar-case paragraph, and sentence following (1.3)).",
      "Real spatial variable x and real time variable t, inherited from q : R × R → R^m (printed page 1, paragraph following (1.1))."
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The velocity ū is constant (printed page 1, paragraph preceding (1.2)).",
      "Set q(x,t) = q̃(x − ūt) (printed page 1, (1.3)); no explicit smoothness hypothesis on q̃ accompanies the assertion."
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "The function q given by (1.3) solves q_t(x,t) + ū q_x(x,t) = 0, subject to the unresolved interpretation of derivatives for an arbitrary profile (printed page 1, sentence introducing (1.3) and equation (1.2)).",
      "The profile propagates at the stated constant speed ū with unchanged shape (printed page 1, sentence following (1.3))."
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "Subscripts x and t denote partial derivatives with respect to space and time (printed page 1, paragraph following (1.1)).",
      "q is scalar concentration or density in the example; q̃ specifies its concentration profile or waveform (printed page 1, paragraphs surrounding (1.2)–(1.3)).",
      "The translated profile is evaluated at x − ūt, with the minus sign fixed by (1.3) (printed page 1, (1.3))."
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The ambient discussion supplies q on R × R; the selected passage specifies no smaller spatial or temporal domain and no boundary condition (printed page 1, paragraph following (1.1) and (1.2)–(1.3)).",
      "Chapter 2 is cited for the derivation of the advection equation; the selected passage separately asserts the translated-profile solution formula (printed page 1, sentence between (1.2) and (1.3))."
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Scope of the solution assertion",
        "details": [
          "The wording 'admits solutions of the form' asserts a family of solutions; it does not explicitly assert uniqueness or that every solution has this form (printed page 1, sentence introducing (1.3))."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Translation and shape preservation",
        "details": [
          "The expression changes only the profile's argument, with no amplitude factor or deformation (printed page 1, (1.3) and following propagation sentence).",
          "As an algebraic consequence, q(x,0) = q̃(x); this is an analyst inference from (1.3), not a separately stated initial-value condition (printed page 1, (1.3))."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Velocity restrictions",
        "details": [
          "The passage imposes constancy but no explicit positivity or nonzero restriction on ū (printed page 1, scalar real-coefficient context and paragraph preceding (1.2))."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Retain the scalar constant-coefficient equation q_t + ūq_x = 0 and the profile argument x − ūt (printed page 1, (1.2)–(1.3)).",
    "Retain the claim of unchanged profile shape under propagation (printed page 1, sentence following (1.3)).",
    "Do not present differentiability as an explicit source hypothesis: the passage says 'any function' without qualifying its regularity (printed page 1, sentence following (1.3)).",
    "Do not strengthen the asserted solution family into uniqueness or an exhaustive classification of solutions (printed page 1, 'admits solutions of the form' preceding (1.3))."
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "Profile regularity and meaning of solution are unspecified.",
      "evidence": "Printed page 1 defines subscripts as partial derivatives, but follows (1.3) with 'for any function q̃(ξ)' and gives no solution-space qualification.",
      "impact": "A classical interpretation requires appropriate differentiability; a generalized interpretation requires a specified solution notion and appropriate assumptions. Neither choice should be silently attributed to this passage."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The text uses both velocity and speed for ū.",
      "evidence": "Printed page 1 calls ū a constant velocity before (1.2), then calls it constant speed after (1.3), without imposing a sign restriction.",
      "impact": "The formula fixes displacement as ūt. The wording does not justify adding ū ≥ 0; interpreting speed as a nonnegative magnitude would require distinguishing |ū| from the signed velocity."
    }
  ],
  "contract_plain_english": "For a constant real transport velocity ū, the source asserts that an arbitrary scalar profile q̃ gives an advection solution q(x,t) = q̃(x − ūt), and that this profile propagates without changing shape (printed page 1, equations (1.2)–(1.3) and adjoining sentences). The surrounding discussion supplies real space and time variables and partial-derivative notation (paragraph following (1.1)). The passage does not specify the regularity or solution interpretation needed for its phrase 'any function', and it does not assert uniqueness or classify all solutions."
